# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile for conclav.io — Caddy owns 80/443 on the box, terminates
# TLS for the dev instances by name, and proxies PLAIN HTTP to each gateway's
# web listener on loopback. This file is the source of truth; the on-box copy is
# installed from it (deploy/README.md → "Behind Caddy").
#
# The pairing on the gateway side is the instance.env of each box:
#   WEB_BIND=127.0.0.1                       the listener Caddy proxies to
#   WEB_TLS=# ...                            no native TLS (plain HTTP)
#   WEB_TRUSTED_PROXIES=["127.0.0.1"]        believe X-Forwarded-For from Caddy
# Caddy's reverse_proxy sets X-Forwarded-For (and -Proto, -Host) on every
# request, and since 2.7 it does not trust the header a client sends it unless
# that client is a configured trusted proxy — none is — so the address the
# gateway reads is the connecting client's. WebSocket upgrades (/ws) are proxied
# as-is.
#
# `tls internal` issues from Caddy's own CA (browsers warn, like the old
# self-signed cert did). Once port 80 is reachable from the internet, delete
# that line and Caddy obtains and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate itself.

(game) {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy http://127.0.0.1:{args[0]}
}

dev1.conclav.io {
	import game 6790
}

dev2.conclav.io {
	import game 6793
}
